    {
     "text": "CURTIS ISLAND",
     "label": "a new home, offered to a whole nation",
     "on": "Curtis",
     "kind": "place"
    }
   ],
   "chapter": "The offer",
   "mood": "tense",
   "date": "1964"
  },
  {
   "say": "The Nauruans said no. They did not want to become Australians on someone else's island. They wanted their own country... and their own phosphate.",
   "visuals": [
    {
     "type": "image",
     "search": [
      "Nauru people 1960s"
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "video",
     "search": [
      "waves crashing rocks"
     ],
     "source": "stock"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "say": "But the Nauruans did something no one expected from a few thousand islanders. They fought back in the one place the big powers respected. At the United Nations.",
   "visuals": [
    {
     "type": "image",
     "search": [
      "United Nations Trusteeship Council 1960s",
      "United Nations Trusteeship Council chamber"
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "image",
     "search": [
      "Hammer DeRoburt"
     ],
     "caption": "Hammer DeRoburt"
    }
   ],
   "chapter": "Independence",
   "mood": "hopeful",
   "date": "1966"
  },
  {
   "say": "Their leader, Hammer DeRoburt, a former teacher, argued for years. And on January thirty first, nineteen sixty eight, Nauru became the smallest independent republic in the world.",
   "visuals": [
    {
     "type": "image",
     "search": [
      "Hammer DeRoburt portrait"
     ],
     "caption": "Hammer DeRoburt, first president"
    },
    {
     "type": "date",
     "text": "January 31, 1968",
